        statute: {
          citation: "18 U.S.C. 1348, text containing those laws in effect on August 13, 2026, and Florida Statutes 517.301 and 517.302, the 2025 Florida Statutes",
          body: "18 U.S.C. 1348, Securities and commodities fraud, reads: Whoever knowingly executes, or attempts to execute, a scheme or artifice, (1) to defraud any person in connection with any commodity for future delivery, or any option on a commodity for future delivery, or any security of an issuer with a class of securities registered under section 12 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (15 U.S.C. 78l) or that is required to file reports under section 15(d) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (15 U.S.C. 78o(d)); or (2) to obtain, by means of false or fraudulent pretenses, representations, or promises, any money or property in connection with the purchase or sale of any commodity for future delivery, or any option on a commodity for future delivery, or any security of an issuer with a class of securities registered under section 12 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (15 U.S.C. 78l) or that is required to file reports under section 15(d) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (15 U.S.C. 78o(d)); shall be fined under this title, or imprisoned not more than 25 years, or both. That is the section. It states no dollar threshold, no mandatory minimum, and no exception clause of its own. Florida splits the same job across two sections, and reading the first without the second produces a wrong answer. Statute 517.301 is titled Fraudulent transactions; falsification or concealment of facts, and it is prohibitory and definitional: subsection (1) states what is unlawful, subsection (2) defines investment for that section, subsections (3) through (5) reach misrepresentations that a security or a person has been guaranteed, sponsored, recommended, or approved by the state or the United States or an agency or officer of either, subsection (6) governs statements that a person or a security is registered, providing at (6)(a) that subsection (3) or subsection (4) may not be construed to prohibit a statement that a person or security is registered or has made a notice filing under the chapter if that statement is required by the chapter or rules promulgated thereunder and is true in fact and if the effect of the statement is not a misrepresentation, and prescribing at (6)(b) a disclaimer that must be included in a statement that a person is registered made in connection with the offer or sale of a security under the chapter, and subsection (7) reaches managing, supervising, controlling, or owning a boiler room in this state. It assigns no degree of offense. The degree comes from Statute 517.302, titled Criminal penalties; alternative fine; Anti-Fraud Trust Fund; time limitation for criminal prosecution, whose subsection (1) makes a violation of any of the provisions of the chapter a felony of the third degree and whose subsection (2) makes a violation of Statute 517.301 by obtaining money or property of an aggregate value exceeding $50,000 from five or more persons a felony of the first degree.",
        },
